In article <1994Sep24.172225.7201@gov.nt.ca>, Jim Grant <jimg@gov.nt.ca> wrote:
>Interlog and Bruce Lloyd have given Canada an opportunity to test
>up to three important 'Net concepts before the courts, and of course
>the news media.

Jim Grant comments on three issues:
  freedom of expression, liability for content, right of universal access
as they relate to Usenet News.

He suggests we let the courts decide how the law applies to cyberspace.
I tend to disagree.  I think current Canadian laws are inadequate and vague.


1. I would argue that the right to freedom of expression, which goes
   hand-in-hand with the freedom to read is something we are already
   guaranteed under the Canadian Charter.  The "medium" is irrelevant.
   It is the "message" that counts.  These are individual rights
   and it is up to individuals to stand up and defend them.

2. The telephone companies and the post office are not liable for
   the "content" they deliver.  They are granted common carrier
   status which excuses them from any such liability, but in return
   they must provide service to all comers.  Hence, protection
   from liability and universal access are intertwined.

One of the stated goals of Electronic Frontier Canada is to
work for the establishment of:

* a policy of common carriage requirements for all network providers
  so that all forms of speech and expression, no matter how
  controversial, will be carried without discrimination.

>Jim Grant says:
>In conclusion I can only say that these things need to go through
>our legal system, and that until they do all of us who participate
>in the internet in Canada do so with UNWARRANTED risk.

I agree there is a risk.  My suggestion is that those of us who have
a stake should become involved in the formulation of the new laws
to eliminate or at least reduce the risk.  This is too important an issue
to let ill-informed lawyers and judges decide for us based on antiquated laws.

David Jones

