djones@CIM.McGill.CA (David Jones) writes:
>1. I would argue that the right to freedom of expression, which goes
>   hand-in-hand with the freedom to read is something we are already
>   guaranteed under the Canadian Charter.  The "medium" is irrelevant.

Wrong on two points. The medium is relevant. Newspapers are not common
carriers, and they refuse to carry material every day. "Common carrier"
is a phrase typically used to refer to private communications, not to
discourse in a broadcast medium. 

Point 2: The charter applies at its weakest to dealings between private
individuals for non-essential services. The charter does not apply to
legal contracts between private individuals.

>* a policy of common carriage requirements for all network providers
>  so that all forms of speech and expression, no matter how
>  controversial, will be carried without discrimination.

Wrong again. The phone company is a common carrier, yet obscene phone
callers are not allowed. I quote Edmonton Telehones' rules:

 "The customer shall not use ED TEL's services, nor permit them to be 
  used, for the purpose of making annoying, profane, harassing,
  abusive, obscene or offensive calls."

It doesn't take a judge or a jury long to tell the difference between
abusive calls and controversial opinions. If you want for EMail and
NetNews what the telephone system has, you are not asking for an
improvement. You are unknowingly asking for a greater restriction on
the use of EMail and NetNews than already exists.

>This is too important an issue to let ill-informed 
>lawyers and judges decide for us based on antiquated laws.
>David Jones

I haven't read such an arrogant statement in a long time. Since most
lawyers and judges have formal legal training, and deal with the law
every day, it seems to me that YOU are the ill-informed one.
-- 
