nichols@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu (Steve Nichols) writes:

>I just heard on NPR today about a sexual harassment case at a California
>community college.

I think there are two parts to the suit.

 Part 1 says that it violates Title IX (I think) to have
  school-sponsored sex-segregated forums on campus. I think this is
  the part that the school settled. Interestingly, a women-only
  computer forum was started first. The men's forum was an
  afterthought added in an attempt to avoid violating Title IX. But my
  understanding is that Title IX only allows segregation in dorms and
  sports. Men-only (or women-only) university-registered organizations
  are illegal.  University created women-only (or men-only) counseling
  groups may be illegal.

  In other words, my understanding is that university-sponsored
  sex-segregated computer forums are illegal. Note that this shouldn't
  affect independent forums like the the Syster's mailing list because
  they are not university-sponsored.

Part 2 of the suit alleges (I understand) that comments made about the
  women in the men-only forum illegally created a sexually hostile
  environment and that the University was responsible because 1) It created
  the forums 2) It appointed a "moderator" for the forum.

  This part has not been decided yet. I think the result would depend
  on 1) the affect of conceding that segregation was itself illegal and
  2) how much the "moderator" was an agent of the University.

- Carl
